WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
(audience clapping)

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:01.666
- All right, we also
have another actress here

00:00:01.666 --> 00:00:03.610
from the cast of "The Thing About Pam",

00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:05.880
which you can see Tuesdays on NBC.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.990
She was supposed to tape her
interview with us earlier,

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:09.900
but got stuck in a snowstorm,

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:11.270
which I have been stuck in a snowstorm

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:12.290
and it sucks.

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:13.990
She's a two-time Oscar winner

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:15.940
who hails from the best
state in the union, y'all.

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:17.090
That's right, we're annoying.

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:17.950
Texas.

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:18.878
That's right.

00:00:18.878 --> 00:00:19.711
(audience clapping)
(audience cheering)

00:00:19.711 --> 00:00:22.678
Please welcome fellow Texas
girl, Renée Zellweger! (claps)

00:00:22.678 --> 00:00:23.511
(upbeat music)

00:00:23.511 --> 00:00:28.511
(audience cheering)
(audience clapping)

00:00:35.102 --> 00:00:35.935
- Hi, y'all!

00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:36.768
(audience clapping)
(audience cheering)

00:00:36.768 --> 00:00:37.601
- [Kelly] It's nice to see-

00:00:37.601 --> 00:00:38.434
- Hi.

00:00:38.434 --> 00:00:39.267
- [Kelly] Oh, you look so beautiful.

00:00:39.267 --> 00:00:40.100
- Thank you. So do you, ma'am.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:41.500
- [Kelly] I love this
whole Audrey Hepburn look.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:42.600
- Oh, thanks so much.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:43.810
- How are you doing?

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:45.500
I've been stuck in a snowstorm before

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:46.333
(Renee laughs)

00:00:46.333 --> 00:00:47.440
and without much.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:48.273
- [Renée] You know?

00:00:48.273 --> 00:00:49.106
- Yeah, Yeah.

00:00:49.106 --> 00:00:50.320
- And there's not a lot you can do.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:52.270
You just kinda have to resign to it.

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:53.500
There's not a lot you can do.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:56.393
- So how far did you make it
till you had to pull over?

00:00:56.393 --> 00:00:58.380
- Well, I was just outside of Bakersfield.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:00:59.480
I'd come down from Davis,

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:01.780
there's this great
veterinarian hospital up there.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:04.910
That's where my fella goes
to get his hip surgeries

00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:06.200
and whatnot.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:07.524
He just got outta surgery now, actually.

00:01:07.524 --> 00:01:08.357
- [Kelly] Oh, wow.

00:01:08.357 --> 00:01:09.190
- So, I know, fingers crossed.

00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:11.356
I just got word he has a herniated disc-

00:01:11.356 --> 00:01:12.189
- [Kelly] Oh my gosh.

00:01:12.189 --> 00:01:13.022
- it seems.

00:01:13.022 --> 00:01:13.855
- Me too.

00:01:13.855 --> 00:01:15.339
In my neck.
- Right? (laughs)

00:01:15.339 --> 00:01:16.172
- Yeah.

00:01:16.172 --> 00:01:17.005
- I know a really good surgeon. (laughs)

00:01:17.005 --> 00:01:17.838
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:17.838 --> 00:01:20.110
So, yeah, so Bakersfield,

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:22.330
and you just see all the
18 wheelers pulling over

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:23.340
into the right-hand lanes

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:25.740
and it was bumper to bumper for miles.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:27.690
You could see what was going on.

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:31.310
The maps, and the Waze, and the
maps and they try to divert.

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:32.950
They're like, "Okay, go, you know

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:34.200
take this alternative route."

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.790
And it was heading into
the mountains. (laughs)

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:38.100
- And you were like, wrong turn. (laughs)

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:41.330
- In my rented minivan and a T-shirt

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:42.830
and I'm like, "Nah."

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:44.000
- My rented minivan.
- Nah.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.680
- And a T-shirt. (laughs)

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:46.831
- Yeah.

00:01:46.831 --> 00:01:47.664
- Oh my god, I love that.

00:01:47.664 --> 00:01:48.650
It's always good to
have a Texas girl here,

00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:50.800
but you're from, not Houston, is it Katy?

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:51.715
- Yeah, yeah.
- Yeah.

00:01:51.715 --> 00:01:53.780
A couple of my band
members are from Beaumont,

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:54.638
that whole area.

00:01:54.638 --> 00:01:55.471
- Oh, yeah, sure.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:01:55.471 --> 00:01:58.180
- I actually went to elementary school

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:00.120
on the east side of there near Beaumont,

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:00.953
so I got over there.

00:02:00.953 --> 00:02:01.981
- [Kelly] Oh, right on.

00:02:01.981 --> 00:02:02.814
- [Renée] Yeah.

00:02:02.814 --> 00:02:03.940
- Do you get back there often?

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:05.370
- The last time I was there, I think...

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:06.571
I drive through a lot, you know?

00:02:06.571 --> 00:02:07.404
- Yeah.

00:02:07.404 --> 00:02:08.840
- 'Cause I'll drive out to Austin,

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:10.340
I like to road trip,

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:11.540
and I'll head down occasionally.

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:14.140
I went to my fifth grade
teacher's retirement

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:16.845
a couple years ago, Mr. Matson, and-

00:02:16.845 --> 00:02:17.678
- Oh my god, I love that.

00:02:17.678 --> 00:02:19.613
- Yeah, he became the
high school librarian,

00:02:19.613 --> 00:02:23.730
so he was with us until my class,

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:25.860
till we graduated, and, yeah,
- That's cool.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:27.590
- I couldn't recognize my town.

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:28.667
It grew so much.

00:02:28.667 --> 00:02:29.500
It was crazy.
- Oh my gosh, me too.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:30.670
- Yeah?
I left and I came back.

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:32.010
It was just like even a few years later,

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:33.840
and I was like, "This
is like a metropolis."

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:34.699
- [Renee] Yeah, it's crazy.

00:02:34.699 --> 00:02:35.532
- Like, what?

00:02:35.532 --> 00:02:36.365
We had, like, a Walmart.
- There's overpasses.

00:02:36.365 --> 00:02:37.198
- That was all we had
(Renee laughs)

00:02:37.198 --> 00:02:38.160
when I was here.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:39.320
- What happened?
- Yeah.

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:40.153
- We got a mall.

00:02:40.153 --> 00:02:41.776
- You got real cool when I left.

00:02:41.776 --> 00:02:42.609
(Kelly laughs)

00:02:42.609 --> 00:02:43.442
I was like, "Whatever."

00:02:43.442 --> 00:02:44.490
What's the hardest part though for you

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:45.350
for getting used to LA?

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:46.720
Mine is traffic.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:47.570
- Oh, interesting.

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:49.110
Okay, it was that crosswalk.

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:50.740
You know the crosswalk where...

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:52.470
'Cause usually if somebody
stops in the left lane,

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:53.810
it's 'cause they're gonna turn left,

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:55.410
and so you'll go around them.

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:58.921
But no, no, in LA it's
because there's a human.

00:02:58.921 --> 00:02:59.780
(both laugh)

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:01.057
There's a human in the road

00:03:01.057 --> 00:03:03.300
and you have to learn not
to go around the person.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:04.480
- [Kelly] To not hit the pedestrians.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:05.950
- Correct, don't kill anybody.

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:07.260
That was the big part.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:08.093
- That's a good lesson.

00:03:08.093 --> 00:03:09.102
- Don't kill anybody.

00:03:09.102 --> 00:03:10.920
That and the billboards.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:13.030
Do you remember that,
when you got to town?

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:14.650
The enormity of them?

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:15.900
You drive down Sunset Boulevard.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:17.590
Now I'm sure anybody who's visiting,

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:18.990
it's remarkable, isn't it?

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:20.010
- It's kind of overwhelming though.

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:21.830
- It is, they're all layered.

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:22.752
It looks kind of like it's-

00:03:22.752 --> 00:03:24.280
- Yeah, I literally, when I
moved here after high school,

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:25.810
I was like 18 when I moved here,

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:26.950
and it was right after high school,

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:28.020
and I remember going on Sunset,

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:28.853
and I was like,

00:03:28.853 --> 00:03:30.980
there was almost too much happening.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:31.813
I couldn't.

00:03:31.813 --> 00:03:33.310
And then I'm like, "How do
people not wreck daily here

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:34.364
just looking at everything?"

00:03:34.364 --> 00:03:35.512
- Yeah, 'cause it's a stimuli.
- Yeah.

00:03:35.512 --> 00:03:36.890
- A stimuli.
- Yeah.

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:38.580
- Yeah, it's a lot.
- Yeah, it's overwhelming.

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:39.413
It's true,

00:03:39.413 --> 00:03:41.440
and it feels like, it's almost
like, there's so much noise

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:42.920
that they gotta get bigger, and louder,

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:43.753
and bigger, and louder,

00:03:43.753 --> 00:03:45.508
and nobody notices it
at all, or something.

00:03:45.508 --> 00:03:46.341
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:46.341 --> 00:03:47.174
- I don't know.

00:03:47.174 --> 00:03:48.020
- And do you want them to notice it

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:48.853
while they're behind the wheel?

00:03:48.853 --> 00:03:49.790
I don't know.
- Yeah, there's that too.

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:50.623
- Then there's that part.

00:03:50.623 --> 00:03:51.804
- I don't know.

00:03:51.804 --> 00:03:52.637
Do you remember though,

00:03:52.637 --> 00:03:53.470
the first time your
face was on a billboard?

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:54.570
(Renée laughs)

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:55.596
'Cause that's fun.

00:03:55.596 --> 00:03:58.620
I remember mine, it was like
a radio station, I think.

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:00.070
They put my face with another artist,

00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:01.010
or whatever for a radio station.

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:03.250
I was like, "My face is on a giant board."

00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:05.120
I was like, "I have made it."

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:07.596
- I remember the first time
your face was on a giant board.

00:04:07.596 --> 00:04:08.836
(both laugh)

00:04:08.836 --> 00:04:10.346
- Do you remember your first?

00:04:10.346 --> 00:04:11.460
- A lot of us remember the first time

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:12.548
your face was on a giant board. (claps)

00:04:12.548 --> 00:04:13.381
(audience clapping)

00:04:13.381 --> 00:04:14.214
- Aww, you're so nice.

00:04:14.214 --> 00:04:15.047
- I was so happy for you.
- I just thought

00:04:15.047 --> 00:04:15.880
it was really cool.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:16.713
I was like, "Wow."

00:04:16.713 --> 00:04:17.621
I dunno why they wanted me
up there, but it happened.

00:04:17.621 --> 00:04:19.027
- Well we know why. (laughs)

00:04:19.027 --> 00:04:21.170
- Wait, what Was your experience like?

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:22.020
Do you remember?

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:25.067
- Well, I just remember that
I had a lot of films come out,

00:04:25.067 --> 00:04:27.070
a bunch of films come out,

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:29.830
and they didn't need me to
be on the billboard (laughs).

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:31.978
- Oh my God, so it's like
the other person's on the-

00:04:31.978 --> 00:04:33.848
- I barely made the movie.

00:04:33.848 --> 00:04:34.818
- Oh my gosh.

00:04:34.818 --> 00:04:35.920
- I barely made the movie, so-

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:37.116
- Well look at you now.

00:04:37.116 --> 00:04:38.257
- They didn't need me on the billboard.

00:04:38.257 --> 00:04:39.090
(audience clapping)
- Okay, okay.

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:39.923
- How sweet.

00:04:39.923 --> 00:04:42.440
- Do you, whenever, I know
you said you go to Austin,

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:43.470
do you miss Texas food?

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:44.629
I love Texas food.

00:04:44.629 --> 00:04:47.450
- It's funny, when I make
my plans with my friends,

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:49.433
it's always around where
we're gonna eat. (laughs)

00:04:49.433 --> 00:04:50.840
- Why not? Absolutely.

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:52.790
- How many times we could cram tacos

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:53.700
into the schedule,

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:55.397
that's basically-
- Yes, 'cause it's Tex-Mex.

00:04:55.397 --> 00:04:56.510
- 'Cause it's Tex-Mex.

00:04:56.510 --> 00:04:57.950
- Yes, It's different than here.

00:04:57.950 --> 00:04:58.997
So when I moved here,

00:04:58.997 --> 00:04:59.831
I was like, "What is this Mexican food?"

00:04:59.831 --> 00:05:00.932
It's probably authentic Mexican food.

00:05:00.932 --> 00:05:02.560
- Why is there mango in my salsa? (laughs)

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:03.393
- I know.

00:05:03.393 --> 00:05:04.780
I was like, "Why? Why?

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:07.000
And there's strawberries,
is that new for guac?

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:07.833
Okay, cool."

00:05:07.833 --> 00:05:10.690
- And why is my iced tea
passion fruit? Like what?

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:12.274
- [Kelly] Yeah, I just want a normal one.

00:05:12.274 --> 00:05:13.107
- I just want a lemon.

00:05:13.107 --> 00:05:15.320
- Yeah, I love Tex-Mex, so
that's my favorite thing.

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:17.764
And I thought that
maybe you might miss it.

00:05:17.764 --> 00:05:18.980
- I do miss-
So I brought you something.

00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:19.813
- [Renée] What?

00:05:19.813 --> 00:05:20.646
- Okay, here we go.

00:05:20.646 --> 00:05:21.479
- What?

00:05:21.479 --> 00:05:22.312
- I'm a polite host
(audience clapping)

00:05:22.312 --> 00:05:23.145
I'm a polite host.

00:05:23.145 --> 00:05:24.364
I'm a polite host.

00:05:24.364 --> 00:05:25.380
(audience cheering)
You have a drink

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:26.766
and some queso.

00:05:26.766 --> 00:05:27.599
- [Renée] I dunno.

00:05:27.599 --> 00:05:28.432
- Okay, no, no, wait.

00:05:28.432 --> 00:05:29.265
- You're gonna have to
wrap this up. (laughs)

00:05:29.265 --> 00:05:30.680
- Hold on, sit down for a minute, wait.

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:31.513
'Cause here's the thing.

00:05:31.513 --> 00:05:32.530
I have been...

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:33.690
This is season three.

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:35.380
Since season one, I have been searching

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:37.160
for good queso in LA.

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:37.993
- [Renée] Okay.

00:05:37.993 --> 00:05:38.850
This queso is from Roadside Taco,

00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:41.700
right here in Studio City.
- Oh, very nice.

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:43.340
- Shout out because it's awesome.

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:44.173
- Okay.

00:05:44.173 --> 00:05:45.060
- I'm not even being paid by them,

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:46.340
I just really love this queso.

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:47.370
- It does look good, Roadside.

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:48.896
- But I need you to like,
maybe, do you want a taste?

00:05:48.896 --> 00:05:49.729
- No, no, I'm in.

00:05:49.729 --> 00:05:51.060
- Okay, We have our own.
- I'm so in.

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:51.893
- We have our own.

00:05:51.893 --> 00:05:52.726
- Is this mean?

00:05:52.726 --> 00:05:54.008
'Cause everybody's leaning
forward over there.

00:05:54.008 --> 00:05:54.841
Is this mean?
- I don't know,

00:05:54.841 --> 00:05:55.674
but my mouth is watering,

00:05:55.674 --> 00:05:56.652
and I'm so excited.

00:05:56.652 --> 00:05:57.510
- I know, I'm a little happy about it.

00:05:57.510 --> 00:06:00.240
- Oh you guys, I'd like
to apologize in advance,

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:01.799
'cause these look really good.

00:06:01.799 --> 00:06:02.883
(audience laughing)

00:06:02.883 --> 00:06:03.970
- It's good.

00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:05.180
It needs to be warmed up a tiny bit,

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:07.470
but whatever, it's really good.

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:08.303
- I mean.

00:06:08.303 --> 00:06:09.840
- But I like it when
it's warmed up heavily,

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:11.959
it's really runny, that's what I love.

00:06:11.959 --> 00:06:13.878
- You know what?
- And a margarita.

00:06:13.878 --> 00:06:14.999
- I think it's better than that.

00:06:14.999 --> 00:06:16.630
My head might blow off.

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:17.880
It is so good.

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:19.490
- I know, it's so hard to
find good queso here, but-

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:20.470
- Thank you, cheers.

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:21.303
- Cheers.

00:06:21.303 --> 00:06:22.778
- Oh, I drank before, that's rude.

00:06:22.778 --> 00:06:23.658
(audience clapping)

00:06:23.658 --> 00:06:24.707
Oh, I like your ring.

00:06:24.707 --> 00:06:26.360
- Ladies and gentlemen, Kelly Clarkson.

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:28.109
Thank you, what a sweet thing to do.

00:06:28.109 --> 00:06:29.880
- Congrats on this series.

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:32.207
So this was actually inspired
by a Dateline episode, right?

00:06:32.207 --> 00:06:33.670
- Yeah, it is.

00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:35.710
It's based on the Dateline episode

00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:37.629
and the subsequent podcast,

00:06:37.629 --> 00:06:40.207
which I actually listened to

00:06:40.207 --> 00:06:43.270
on the 5 freeway, driving Chester the dog

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:44.658
up to get his hip surgery. (laughs)

00:06:44.658 --> 00:06:45.940
- [Kelly] His name is Chester?

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:46.773
- His name is Chester.

00:06:46.773 --> 00:06:48.050
- Oh, my dog's name is Henry.

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:48.883
- Oh see.

00:06:48.883 --> 00:06:49.820
- I feel like they'd be friends.

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:51.767
- Chester doesn't really
have friendly manners.

00:06:51.767 --> 00:06:52.600
- You know what?

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:53.433
Neither does...

00:06:53.433 --> 00:06:54.698
Henry doesn't have dog friends either.

00:06:54.698 --> 00:06:55.531
(Renée laughs)

00:06:55.531 --> 00:06:56.687
- Yeah, he doesn't.
- I wish.

00:06:56.687 --> 00:06:57.520
- I know.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:06:59.320
- I wish, no, I don't know what it is.

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:01.618
He just thinks that they
either make good snacks,

00:07:01.618 --> 00:07:02.451
(Kelly laughs)

00:07:02.451 --> 00:07:04.174
or like that they're really da- you know?

00:07:04.174 --> 00:07:05.440
- Yeah.
- He's gotta protect me

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:06.910
from all the dogs in the world.

00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:07.850
So, you know-

00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:08.683
- I love that.

00:07:08.683 --> 00:07:10.050
- He's like a, yeah, I know, but-

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:10.883
- I love that.

00:07:10.883 --> 00:07:11.790
So you would listen to the podcast?

00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:13.692
- Oh, yeah, I listened to the podcast,

00:07:13.692 --> 00:07:14.525
and I binged it,

00:07:14.525 --> 00:07:15.900
and there wasn't even a snowstorm.

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:19.700
I crammed it in there
on the way up to Davis,

00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:21.500
and I thought, "Wow,
that's really interesting."

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:23.624
'Cause you start listening to the story.

00:07:23.624 --> 00:07:26.360
It revolves around the case

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:29.190
of the murder of Betsy Faria in 2011.

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:32.520
And you just start to ask questions,

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:33.850
like how did this happen?

00:07:33.850 --> 00:07:37.070
and how did her husband get convicted

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:38.470
when there was so much evidence

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:40.040
that made you scratch your head about it?

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:41.220
- Yeah.
- And it just seemed

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:44.100
like something that would be
really interesting to explore.

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:46.060
- Yeah, it's a crazy story.

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:46.893
- It's a crazy story.

00:07:46.893 --> 00:07:49.473
- Yes, and is it fun to get
to play a part like that?

00:07:50.690 --> 00:07:52.140
- Well-

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:53.137
- Or do you take some of it home?

00:07:53.137 --> 00:07:55.000
Like when you go in
that character? (laughs)

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:56.217
- Oh no. (laughs)

00:07:56.217 --> 00:07:59.641
- Yeah, you're like, "No,
but ask others." (laughs)

00:07:59.641 --> 00:08:00.610
- Yeah, right.

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:02.850
No, I like the research, you know?

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:03.683
- Yeah.

00:08:03.683 --> 00:08:04.516
- Because there's something about it

00:08:04.516 --> 00:08:06.220
that's compelling, something about it

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:07.680
that's difficult to understand,

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:08.680
and putting the pieces together,

00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:11.580
to try to paint a picture that
makes it a little bit more,

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:14.910
that makes it makes sense,
is what's interesting to me.

00:08:14.910 --> 00:08:18.470
And so, lots of studying
about human behavior,

00:08:18.470 --> 00:08:21.280
and what motivates people
to do certain things,

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:23.130
and just like that.
- Yeah.

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:25.130
So wait, how much do you lose of yourself

00:08:25.130 --> 00:08:25.963
in these characters?

00:08:25.963 --> 00:08:28.370
'Cause I've heard it's
one of my favorite things.

00:08:28.370 --> 00:08:29.550
I think it was Sting.

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:30.670
I might be not saying this right.

00:08:30.670 --> 00:08:32.690
But whenever he goes
into writing like music,

00:08:32.690 --> 00:08:35.450
it's hard to get out of it.

00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:36.283
You get so into it.

00:08:36.283 --> 00:08:37.120
You wanna make the record great.

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:38.240
You wanna make the song great.

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:40.270
And it's hard to kind of
climb out, like emotionally.

00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:41.952
Do you ever do that with characters?

00:08:41.952 --> 00:08:44.629
- Well, not on purpose, you know?

00:08:44.629 --> 00:08:45.462
- Yeah.

00:08:45.462 --> 00:08:47.360
- It's just that you take
on all of this information,

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:49.070
and you're processing it and living it.

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:50.250
You're living in it all the time

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:52.333
and then reenacting it during the day.

00:08:52.333 --> 00:08:54.430
Go home, you read and take in some more.

00:08:54.430 --> 00:08:57.252
So, yeah, it becomes part of...

00:08:57.252 --> 00:09:00.840
It becomes a really
big part of your focus,

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:01.673
your daily focus.

00:09:01.673 --> 00:09:02.673
- [Kelly] For that moment.

00:09:02.673 --> 00:09:03.506
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:09:03.506 --> 00:09:04.339
- That's weird.
- Yeah, when you hear

00:09:04.339 --> 00:09:06.250
the voice, and all the
sayings, all the time.

00:09:06.250 --> 00:09:08.034
So not, yeah,

00:09:08.034 --> 00:09:08.867
(Kelly laughs)

00:09:08.867 --> 00:09:11.572
the character's voice from recordings.

00:09:11.572 --> 00:09:12.430
Not like-

00:09:12.430 --> 00:09:15.370
- You hear voices, and everything's fine.

00:09:15.370 --> 00:09:16.330
(both laugh)

00:09:16.330 --> 00:09:19.350
- So wait, was becoming Pam more difficult

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:20.940
than becoming Judy Garland,

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:22.360
or less intimidating?

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:23.193
I don't know, which one was?

00:09:23.193 --> 00:09:24.820
I feel like it would be
intimidating to play Judy Garland.

00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:25.653
- [Renée] Wow. That's-

00:09:25.653 --> 00:09:26.820
- [Kelly] You nailed it.

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:27.662
- [Renée] Thank you.

00:09:27.662 --> 00:09:28.495
(audience clapping)

00:09:28.495 --> 00:09:29.703
- Yeah, but I just mean
that would be intimidating.

00:09:30.621 --> 00:09:31.454
(audience clapping)

00:09:31.454 --> 00:09:36.170
- Yeah, I try to remove
myself from the reality of it,

00:09:37.030 --> 00:09:38.649
which is that you're creating something

00:09:38.649 --> 00:09:39.770
that folks are gonna see,

00:09:39.770 --> 00:09:42.290
and I just kind of go-

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:43.123
- Dive in.

00:09:43.123 --> 00:09:43.956
- Yeah, that's it.

00:09:43.956 --> 00:09:46.190
Just dive into it and do the work.

00:09:46.190 --> 00:09:48.260
So I just get really involved in the work,

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:49.290
and it becomes about that,

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:50.570
so I don't think about anything else.

00:09:50.570 --> 00:09:51.521
I'm just curious.

00:09:51.521 --> 00:09:52.469
- [Kelly] That's smart.

00:09:52.469 --> 00:09:53.302
- I got from that perspective.

00:09:53.302 --> 00:09:55.669
- But this was, it was hotter.

00:09:55.669 --> 00:09:57.440
It was hotter to play.

00:09:57.440 --> 00:09:58.273
(Kelly laughs)

00:09:58.273 --> 00:09:59.818
- I was miserable.
- That stuff.

00:09:59.818 --> 00:10:00.651
- It was hot.

00:10:00.651 --> 00:10:02.770
No, there was a lot of comedy involved,

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:04.680
'cause you gotta laugh your
way through stuff like that.

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:07.570
But I'd never worked with full prosthetics

00:10:07.570 --> 00:10:08.550
like that before.

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:10.180
And it's a skill that I didn't know

00:10:10.180 --> 00:10:11.230
that I did not have. (laughs)

00:10:11.230 --> 00:10:13.573
- That I was not excited about.

00:10:13.573 --> 00:10:14.820
- I did not know.

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:16.430
It was fun to learn, you know?

00:10:16.430 --> 00:10:17.880
But it's fun on the first day,

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:19.228
when you go, "Oh wait." (laughs)

00:10:19.228 --> 00:10:20.320
- [Kelly] Yeah, that's a lot.

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:22.630
- Wait, I can't, I'm smiling,
but you can't tell. (laughs)

00:10:22.630 --> 00:10:25.710
- Yeah, oh my God, like acting
in that's such a hard thing.

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:26.937
Oh my gosh, I never thought about that.

00:10:26.937 --> 00:10:29.030
- You gotta move these
things and you have to...

00:10:29.030 --> 00:10:30.520
Yeah, but Arian, he's a genius.

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:31.550
- Yeah.
- And yeah, he's really cool.

00:10:31.550 --> 00:10:32.700
- It's incredible.

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:34.058
I call my glam squad, Harry Potter.

00:10:34.058 --> 00:10:35.317
I do not look like this. (laughs)

00:10:35.317 --> 00:10:36.150
(audience laughing)

00:10:36.150 --> 00:10:37.400
I was like, you will see me in Target

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:38.498
and be like, "Wait what?" (laughs)

00:10:38.498 --> 00:10:39.331
(audience laughing)

00:10:39.331 --> 00:10:41.370
But for Judy you got to
record at Abbey Road, I heard.

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:42.203
- Yes.

00:10:42.203 --> 00:10:43.036
- Was that so insane?

00:10:43.036 --> 00:10:43.869
- It's crazy.

00:10:43.869 --> 00:10:44.750
- Yeah.
- I laugh every day.

00:10:44.750 --> 00:10:45.623
- I mean, musicians don't
ever get to do that.

00:10:45.623 --> 00:10:47.130
You know what I'm saying?

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:48.320
Like that's really cool.
- Have you been?

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:49.189
- I've been to Abbey Road,

00:10:49.189 --> 00:10:50.022
yeah, yeah, yeah.
- Yeah, that was I gonna say,

00:10:50.022 --> 00:10:50.855
cause I'm sure that-

00:10:50.855 --> 00:10:51.688
- I've never recorded there.

00:10:51.688 --> 00:10:52.521
That's amazing.

00:10:52.521 --> 00:10:53.570
- It's just a-

00:10:53.570 --> 00:10:55.790
- Yeah, it's childhood dream stuff.

00:10:55.790 --> 00:10:56.623
- Absolutely.

00:10:56.623 --> 00:10:57.456
- Yeah, 'cause I don't
know about you guys,

00:10:57.456 --> 00:10:58.550
I grew up a big Beatles fan,

00:10:58.550 --> 00:11:01.820
and just to go there, and
they like let you in. (laughs)

00:11:01.820 --> 00:11:02.760
- And they like let you in? (laughs)

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:04.710
- Yeah, yeah, I walk up on the steps

00:11:04.710 --> 00:11:06.840
and I laugh 'cause
they're gonna let me in.

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:08.713
Yeah, that was enough,

00:11:08.713 --> 00:11:10.670
but then to get to actually go

00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:13.140
into some of the studios and lay tracks,

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:13.973
that was crazy.

